                    "Design patterns are named, reusable solutions to recurring design problems - a shared vocabulary that lets developers communicate a design decision (\"use a Factory here\") without re-explaining the whole idea each time. Creational patterns manage object creation: Singleton ensures a class has exactly one instance with a single global access point (useful for shared resources like a configuration object, though overused historically and now often replaced by dependency injection for the same effect with better testability); Factory Method and Abstract Factory delegate the decision of exactly which concrete class to instantiate to a subclass or a dedicated factory, decoupling client code from concrete construction; Builder constructs a complex object step-by-step through a fluent interface, useful when a constructor would otherwise need an unwieldy number of parameters, especially many optional ones.\n\n"
