                    ("hard", "What are the tradeoffs of adopting `var` widely versus using explicit types in a large, long-lived codebase?",
                     "`var` reduces visual noise for genuinely obvious types (`var user = new User();`) but can hurt readability when the initializer's type isn't immediately obvious from the right-hand side alone - `var result = process(input);` tells a reader nothing about result's type without navigating to process()'s signature, whereas an explicit type annotation would. The common guidance is to use `var` when the type is already stated clearly on the same line (constructors, obviously-named factory methods) and prefer explicit types when the initializer's return type is opaque or when the explicit type meaningfully documents intent for future readers."),
