                    "Aspect-Oriented Programming (AOP) addresses cross-cutting concerns - logging, security checks, transaction management, caching - that would otherwise need to be repeated across many unrelated classes and methods, cluttering business logic with the same boilerplate everywhere. Spring AOP lets you define this cross-cutting behavior once, as an \"aspect,\" and declaratively specify where it applies via a pointcut expression (e.g. \"every public method in any class under the service package\"), rather than manually inserting the same code at every call site. An \"advice\" is the actual code that runs at a matched point: @Before runs before the matched method, @After runs after (regardless of outcome), @AfterReturning runs only on successful return, @AfterThrowing runs only if an exception propagates, and @Around wraps the entire method call, with full control over whether/how it actually proceeds (able to skip the call entirely, modify arguments, or alter the return value).\n\n"
