                    "Spring's caching abstraction (`spring-boot-starter-cache`) provides a declarative way to cache the result of an expensive method call, without hand-writing cache-check/cache-populate logic inline in every method that could benefit. `@Cacheable` on a method checks a named cache for an entry keyed (by default) on the method's arguments before running the method body at all - a cache hit returns the stored value immediately, skipping the real (often slow/expensive) computation or database call entirely; a cache miss runs the method normally and stores its result for next time. `@CacheEvict` removes an entry (or clears an entire cache) when underlying data changes, and `@CachePut` always runs the method but also updates the cache with the fresh result - together these three cover the core lifecycle of keeping cached values roughly consistent with the real data they represent.\n\n"
