                    ("medium", "What is the difference between a liveness probe and a readiness probe, as it relates to Actuator health groups?",
                     "A liveness probe asks \"is this instance in a broken state that requires restarting it entirely?\" - failing it typically triggers a restart. A readiness probe asks \"is this instance currently able to serve traffic right now?\" - failing it just removes the instance from load-balancer rotation temporarily without restarting it, useful for a state like \"still warming up\" or \"temporarily can't reach a downstream dependency\" that should pause traffic but doesn't mean the instance itself is broken. Actuator supports both as distinct health groups (management.endpoint.health.probes.enabled) specifically to give Kubernetes (or similar) this finer-grained signal instead of one blended health status."),
