                    "The transformer architecture, introduced in 2017 ('Attention Is All You Need'), is the foundation of virtually every modern large language model. Its core innovation is the self-attention mechanism: for each token in a sequence, self-attention computes a weighted combination of every other token's representation, with weights determined by how relevant each other token is to the current one - letting the model directly relate any two positions in a sequence regardless of distance, unlike recurrent architectures (RNNs/LSTMs) that had to propagate information step-by-step through a sequence, losing signal over long distances. Multi-head attention runs several attention computations in parallel with different learned projections, letting the model capture different kinds of relationships (syntactic, semantic, positional) simultaneously rather than being limited to a single attention pattern per layer.\n\n"
                    "A transformer stacks many identical layers, each combining a self-attention sub-layer with a position-wise feed-forward network, plus residual connections and layer normalization that make very deep stacks trainable without vanishing gradients. Since attention itself has no inherent sense of token order (it treats the sequence as an unordered set unless told otherwise), positional encodings are added to each token's representation to inject sequence-order information. Decoder-only transformers (the architecture behind GPT-style models) use masked self-attention, where each position can only attend to earlier positions, not later ones - a constraint that makes autoregressive left-to-right text generation coherent, since the model is trained under exactly the same \"predict the next token given only what came before\" constraint it operates under at inference time."
